\title{The fire-driven invasion model simulated by ForestFireR}
\description{
Reference definition of the model this package simulates: two vegetation
types, a native species and an invader, competing for space in a system
where fire is the process that lets the invader win. The same transition
rules are simulated in three ways -- on an explicit lattice
(\code{\link[=simulate_spatial]{simulate_spatial()}}), as the deterministic well-mixed limit
(\code{\link[=simulate_mean_field]{simulate_mean_field()}}), and as the well-mixed stochastic process at a
finite population size (\code{\link[=simulate_mean_field_stochastic]{simulate_mean_field_stochastic()}}). This page
defines the states, the transitions and every parameter the package's
functions take.
}
\section{States}{
Each site of an \code{L} x \code{L} lattice is in one of five states, coded as in
\link{FF_STATE}:\tabular{llll}{
   code \tab name \tab symbol \tab meaning \cr
   1 \tab \code{native} \tab \eqn{V^{nat}} \tab native vegetation \cr
   2 \tab \code{invader} \tab \eqn{V^{inv}} \tab invading vegetation \cr
   3 \tab \code{fire} \tab \eqn{F} \tab burning \cr
   4 \tab \code{empty} \tab \eqn{\emptyset} \tab unoccupied, never burned \cr
   5 \tab \code{empty_postfire} \tab \eqn{\emptyset^F} \tab unoccupied, left behind by a fire \cr
}


Both unoccupied states can be colonised by a vegetated neighbour, but only
\eqn{\emptyset^F} additionally has the long-range post-fire regrowth
channel (see \code{eta_nat}/\code{eta_inv} below). State \eqn{\emptyset} is produced
only by vegetation death, whose rates the R interface fixes at zero, so it
appears only if the initial landscape contains it.

The simulate functions report the densities \code{n1}..\code{n5} (the fraction of
sites in each state, in the order of the table) plus \code{n0 = n4 + n5}, the
total unoccupied fraction. The five state densities sum to 1 at every
recorded point.
}

\section{Transitions}{
Sites change state in continuous time. On the lattice, \eqn{v_X} is the
number of the site's four nearest neighbours (von Neumann neighbourhood,
periodic boundaries by default) that are in state \eqn{X}:\tabular{lll}{
   transition \tab rate \tab set through \cr
   \eqn{\emptyset \to V^{nat}} \tab \eqn{\lambda_{\emptyset nat}\, v_{nat}} \tab \code{L_01} \cr
   \eqn{\emptyset \to V^{inv}} \tab \eqn{\lambda_{\emptyset inv}\, v_{inv}} \tab \code{L_02} \cr
   \eqn{\emptyset^F \to V^{nat}} \tab \eqn{\lambda_{\emptyset nat}\, v_{nat} + \lambda^{rg}_{nat}} \tab \code{L_01}, \code{eta_nat} \cr
   \eqn{\emptyset^F \to V^{inv}} \tab \eqn{\lambda_{\emptyset inv}\, v_{inv} + \lambda^{rg}_{inv}} \tab \code{L_02}, \code{eta_inv} \cr
   \eqn{V^{nat} \to V^{inv}} \tab \eqn{\lambda_{nat\,inv}\, v_{inv}} \tab \code{L_12} \cr
   \eqn{V^{inv} \to V^{nat}} \tab \eqn{\lambda_{inv\,nat}\, v_{nat}} \tab \code{L_21} \cr
   \eqn{V^{nat} \to F} \tab \eqn{\lambda^{ig}_{nat} + \lambda^{sp}_{nat}\, v_F} \tab \code{Lig_13}, \code{xi_nat} \cr
   \eqn{V^{inv} \to F} \tab \eqn{\lambda^{ig}_{inv} + \lambda^{sp}_{inv}\, v_F} \tab \code{Lig_23}, \code{xi_inv} \cr
   \eqn{F \to \emptyset^F} \tab \eqn{\lambda^{F\emptyset}} \tab \code{L_30} \cr
   \eqn{V \to \emptyset} \tab \eqn{\lambda_{nat\,\emptyset}}, \eqn{\lambda_{inv\,\emptyset}} \tab fixed at 0 \cr
}


Colonisation, competitive replacement and fire spread are local: their
rates are proportional to the matching neighbour count, so an isolated
site is inert to them. Spontaneous ignition, fire burnout and long-range
post-fire regrowth do not depend on the neighbourhood.
}

\section{Dimensionless parametrisation}{
Fire spread and post-fire regrowth are supplied as probabilities rather
than as rate constants, which keeps them comparable across parameter sets:
\itemize{
\item \eqn{\xi_i = \lambda^{sp}_i / (\lambda^{sp}_i + \lambda^{F\emptyset})}
(\code{xi_nat}, \code{xi_inv}) is the probability that a burning neighbour sets a
site of type \eqn{i} alight before that neighbour burns out.
\eqn{\xi = 0.5} is the point where spread and burnout are equally fast.
\item \eqn{\eta_i = \lambda^{rg}_i / (\lambda^{rg}_i + \lambda_{\emptyset nat})}
(\code{eta_nat}, \code{eta_inv}) is the probability that a post-fire site is taken
by species \eqn{i}'s long-range regrowth rather than by ordinary
colonisation. Both species use \eqn{\lambda_{\emptyset nat}} (\code{L_01}) as
the reference rate.
}

\code{\link[=xi2lambda]{xi2lambda()}} and \code{\link[=eta2lambda]{eta2lambda()}} invert both relations,
\eqn{\lambda = \xi/(1-\xi)\,\lambda_{ref}}; every simulate function takes
\verb{xi_*}/\verb{eta_*} and converts internally.

The initial landscape adds one more dimensionless parameter, the
clustering \code{p} of the species placed on it (see \code{\link[=generate_landscape]{generate_landscape()}}).
}

\section{Baseline parameters and the invasion mechanism}{
\code{\link[=default_rates]{default_rates()}} returns the baseline set, with time in years:
\eqn{\lambda_{\emptyset nat} = \lambda_{\emptyset inv} = 0.03},
\eqn{\lambda_{nat\,inv} = 0.005}, \eqn{\lambda_{inv\,nat} = 0.01},
\eqn{\lambda^{F\emptyset} = 10^6}, \eqn{\lambda^{ig}_{nat} = 0},
\eqn{\lambda^{ig}_{inv} = 10^{-4}}, \eqn{\xi_{nat} = 0.5},
\eqn{\xi_{inv} = 0.6}, \eqn{\eta_{nat} = 0}, \eqn{\eta_{inv} = 0.6}.

These values make the competition asymmetric in the native species'
favour: the native replaces the invader twice as fast as the reverse
(\eqn{\lambda_{inv\,nat} = 2 \lambda_{nat\,inv}}), and the two colonise
unoccupied space equally well, so without fire the invader cannot spread.
All of its advantages act through fire: it is the only species that
ignites spontaneously, it carries fire better
(\eqn{\xi_{inv} > \xi_{nat}}), and it is the only one that regrows into
post-fire sites through the long-range channel
(\eqn{\eta_{inv} > \eta_{nat} = 0}). Fire converts native vegetation into
\eqn{\emptyset^F}, which the invader claims preferentially, which raises
the amount of fire-prone vegetation: invasion and fire reinforce each
other. \eqn{\xi_{inv}} and \eqn{\eta_{inv}} are the strength of that
feedback, and are the parameters most analyses vary.

Fire is also much faster than vegetation: with
\eqn{\lambda^{F\emptyset} = 10^6}/year a site burns for about half an
hour, against the decades over which vegetation turns over. A single
outbreak therefore resolves in a sliver of simulated time (see
\code{capture_fire_snapshots} in \code{\link[=simulate_spatial]{simulate_spatial()}} for how to observe one).
}

\section{Mean-field limit}{
\code{\link[=simulate_mean_field]{simulate_mean_field()}} integrates the well-mixed limit of the same
transitions, in which each neighbour count \eqn{v_X} is replaced by the
global density \eqn{n_X}:

\deqn{\dot n_1 = \lambda_{\emptyset nat} n_1 (n_4 + n_5) + \lambda^{rg}_{nat} n_5 + \lambda_{inv\,nat} n_1 n_2 - \lambda_{nat\,inv} n_1 n_2 - (\lambda^{ig}_{nat} + \lambda^{sp}_{nat} n_3) n_1}
\deqn{\dot n_2 = \lambda_{\emptyset inv} n_2 (n_4 + n_5) + \lambda^{rg}_{inv} n_5 + \lambda_{nat\,inv} n_1 n_2 - \lambda_{inv\,nat} n_1 n_2 - (\lambda^{ig}_{inv} + \lambda^{sp}_{inv} n_3) n_2}
\deqn{\dot n_3 = (\lambda^{ig}_{nat} + \lambda^{sp}_{nat} n_3) n_1 + (\lambda^{ig}_{inv} + \lambda^{sp}_{inv} n_3) n_2 - \lambda^{F\emptyset} n_3}
\deqn{\dot n_4 = -(\lambda_{\emptyset nat} n_1 + \lambda_{\emptyset inv} n_2) n_4}
\deqn{\dot n_5 = \lambda^{F\emptyset} n_3 - (\lambda_{\emptyset nat} n_1 + \lambda_{\emptyset inv} n_2) n_5 - (\lambda^{rg}_{nat} + \lambda^{rg}_{inv}) n_5}

(the vegetation death terms, zero in the R interface, are omitted). Fire
enters as a continuous density: \eqn{n_3} settles at
\eqn{(\lambda^{ig}_{nat} n_1 + \lambda^{ig}_{inv} n_2)/(\lambda^{F\emptyset}
- \lambda^{sp}_{nat} n_1 - \lambda^{sp}_{inv} n_2)}, of order
\eqn{10^{-11}} under the baseline parameters -- tiny, but always burning,
so the feedback runs continuously. In the lattice and finite-\code{N} versions
fire is instead a discrete, rare event. That difference, rather than the
presence or absence of space alone, is why the three engines can disagree.
}

\section{The three engines}{
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=simulate_spatial]{simulate_spatial()}} and \code{\link[=simulate_spatial_from_grid]{simulate_spatial_from_grid()}}: the lattice
continuous-time Markov chain, simulated exactly with the Gillespie
algorithm. Boundaries are periodic unless \code{periodic = FALSE}.
\item \code{\link[=simulate_mean_field]{simulate_mean_field()}}: the deterministic well-mixed limit above.
\item \code{\link[=simulate_mean_field_stochastic]{simulate_mean_field_stochastic()}}: the continuous-time Markov chain of
the same well-mixed system with \code{N} individuals. Pairwise reactions have
propensity \eqn{\lambda N_i N_j / N} and first-order reactions
\eqn{\lambda N_i}, so the process has the mean-field equations as its
\code{N} -> infinity limit, with demographic stochasticity and discrete fire
at finite \code{N}. Comparing it against \code{\link[=simulate_spatial]{simulate_spatial()}} at
\eqn{N = L^2} separates the effect of spatial structure from that of
demographic noise.
}
}

\section{Initial landscapes}{
\code{\link[=generate_landscape]{generate_landscape()}} places one species over a background of another
with a prescribed density and clustering \code{p}; \code{\link[=generate_landscape_layers]{generate_landscape_layers()}}
builds landscapes from a sequence of such placements over an arbitrary
fill state. Both grow the pattern cell by cell while steering the
neighbour pair statistics towards the targets set by \code{p}, in the spirit of
the pair-correlation landscape generators of Hiebeler (2000).
}

\section{Implementation notes}{
\itemize{
\item Gillespie event selection keeps the per-site total rates in a binary sum
tree (\code{engine = "tree"}, O(log N) per event). \code{engine = "linear"} is the
cumulative scan over all sites, kept for reproducing runs made with
ForestFireR < 0.7.0.
\item Random numbers come from the engine's own \code{std::mt19937_64}, independent
of R's generator, and seeded per call from each function's \code{seed}
argument (or from the clock when that is \code{NULL}). Set
\code{options(ForestFireR.rng = "legacy")} to select the \code{rand()}
generator of versions up to 0.7.x.
\item The mean-field system is stiff: the fire compartment is updated with its
exact linear solution over each step while the vegetation block is
integrated with classic RK4, which is stable for any practical step size
at \eqn{\lambda^{F\emptyset} = 10^6}.
\item A Gillespie step is applied in full, so the reported \code{time_sim} can
exceed the requested \code{T}, by a wide margin when the total rate is low.
}
}

